      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing the Split: Testing Spatial Repetition Across Two Halves",
        "central_argument": "A filmmaker can test whether framing, camera position, and spatial repetition distinguish the film's two halves by treating the film as a controlled experiment in staging and viewpoint. Rather than relying on remembered details, one should log every shot's camera placement, lens choice, and spatial relation to characters, then compare the first half's patterns against the second's. The critic's claim that the halves are 'similar yet different' provides a hypothesis: the same spatial motifs may recur, but with altered framing, distance, or axis. By isolating background geometry, offscreen cues, and editing rhythms, the filmmaker can determine whether the shift is primarily one of mise-en-scène, cinematography, or montage—and whether the film's structure emerges from repetition with variation rather than from narrative exposition.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Background geometry as viewpoint signature",
          "Focal length and spatial compression",
          "Offscreen space and implied presence",
          "Editing patterns and constructive space",
          "Camera movement and spatial transfer",
          "Axis and screen direction as structural marker"
        ],
