      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Testing Uncertainty: Framing, Eyelines, and Editing in The Thing",
        "central_argument": "A filmmaker can study how The Thing organizes uncertainty and group mistrust by treating every formal claim as a testable hypothesis rather than an observed fact. The film’s paranoid scenario—where any character may be an alien imitation—invites close attention to how framing, eyelines, and editing either sustain or disrupt spatial and temporal coherence. Drawing on continuity editing principles, vector-line analysis, and montage theory, this study proposes that the film may systematically manipulate screen direction, shot duration, and point-of-view structures to destabilize viewer confidence. Each section advances a distinct formal relation: temporal order, graphic discontinuity, rhythmic acceleration, vector-line violation, and subjective camera. By logging specific shot patterns and comparing them against classical norms, the filmmaker can distinguish between deliberate stylistic choices and incidental variation, thereby grounding interpretation in observable evidence.",
