      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Preparing Formal Questions for a Sparse-Criticism Film",
        "central_argument": "When film-specific criticism is sparse, rigorous formal analysis can still begin by converting general craft principles into testable hypotheses. For We Are All Strangers, the available record offers only identity metadata and a handful of attributed critical claims, none of which constitute direct observation. The task, then, is to treat the film as an unknown object and to prepare questions that any attentive viewer can answer through close viewing. By drawing on staging, editing, and continuity frameworks, we can formulate conditional propositions about how the film might organize space, time, and performance. These hypotheses do not assert what the film does; they specify what to look for and how to interpret possible findings. The resulting study becomes a protocol for discovery rather than a set of conclusions, turning the absence of criticism into an opportunity for systematic formal inquiry.",
